<!--
  ============================================================================
  RUT_SumpWalkways.xml           UtinniPatches · authored 2026-09-24
  ============================================================================
  SUMP_WALKWAYS_1 build pass ("Sump walkways, two tiers"). Owner ruling
  (question card, 2026-09-24): duckboards (cheap, foul with tar, burn) and
  glasswalk (never fouls, never full speed, plus rare harmless pratfalls).
  Lives alongside RUT_TarMoat/RUT_Boughway (same authoring style: standalone
  TerrainDef, no ParentName, Light-affordance buildable ground — NOT a
  BridgeBase/Bridgeable terrain, since Soil/Sand carry no Bridgeable
  affordance and a walkway needs to run across ordinary dry ground as well as
  the tar-adjacent margin; crossing the liquid tar itself is FlowWorks'
  canal/gate scope, SUMP_TAR_HYDROLOGY_1, not this item's).

  ── RUT_Duckboards ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  "Full speed when fresh; fouls with tar (accumulating filth slows it until
  scrubbed); flammable" (item spec §1). pathCost 0 = full speed, matching
  WoodPlankFloor/RUT_Boughway's own zero-or-near-zero convention for a
  smooth deliberate floor. filthAcceptanceMask is left UNSET (defaults to
  FilthSourceFlags.Any, Source/Verse/TerrainDef.cs:311) — ordinary floors
  already accept filth; the actual souring comes from
  RUT_TarShallow_FilthAcceptance.xml's amended <generatedFilth> wiring
  (RM_Filth_Tar, pathCost 34) tracked in from natural tar by vanilla's own
  Pawn_FilthTracker, and RM_Filth_Tar being the max-of-things-on-cell cost
  PathGrid.CalculatedCostAt already applies (Source/Verse/AI/PathGrid.cs:120)
  is what actually slows a fouled duckboard cell — no new C# for this half.

  Flammability 1.0 — "flammable — a real liability" (item spec §1), above
  vanilla WoodPlankFloor's own 0.22: brindeth is described (flora roster row
  5, sump_flora_roster_2026-09-24.md) as half-dead scrub wood, and the spec's
  own framing wants duckboards to be a genuine hazard in a biome whose
  defense economy is setting the ground on fire (SUMP_TAR_HYDROLOGY_1
  ruling 5). burnedDef reuses vanilla's own BurnedWoodPlankFloor (real,
  TerrainDef ConfigErrors requires SOME burnedDef when Flammability>0,
  verified Verse/TerrainDef.cs — same requirement RUT_TarMoat.xml's own
  header already documents) rather than inventing a new one — no new art.

  costList: WoodLog, 4/cell — PLACEHOLDER. `RM_Brindeth` (the flora this
  is meant to be built from, flora roster row 5) does not exist yet
  (checked this pass: 0 hits for RM_Brindeth outside design docs and this
  item's own file) — same "flag a placeholder, do not invent the items
  pass's own economy def" posture RUT_TarMoat.xml's own header already
  established for barreled tar. ⚠️ OWED: re-point at real brindeth wood the
  moment that flora ships.

  Texture reuses vanilla WoodFloor (Terrain/Surfaces/WoodFloor), tinted
  greyer/duller than milled WoodPlankFloor's own warm brown to read as
  weathered scrub-wood planking rather than a proper floor — no new art,
  same reuse posture RUT_Boughway/RUT_BoughSoil's own headers already
  establish for terrain (not the Things/ namespace-ownership check
  validate_patch.py enforces for ThingDefs).

  ── RUT_Glasswalk ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  "Never fouls, never needs cleaning, tar-proof; permanently speed-capped
  (~80%, never full speed — his spec), plus rare slip-and-fall" (item
  spec §2). filthAcceptanceMask None is the SAME real engine gate
  RUT_TarShallow_FilthAcceptance.xml's own header documents discovering as
  a BUG on RM_TarShallow (FilthMaker.TerrainAcceptsFilth returns false
  outright when filthAcceptanceMask==None, before any other check,
  RimWorld/FilthMaker.cs) — here it is the deliberate feature: nothing can
  ever stick to glasswalk. Do not "fix" this the way that one was fixed.

  pathCost 3 — CALIBRATED against the engine's own displayed-speed formula,
  not guessed: GenPath.SpeedPercentString (Source/Verse/AI/GenPath.cs:71) is
  `13f / (extraPathTicks + 13f)`, and 13 ticks/cardinal-move is a standard
  human's own TicksPerMoveCardinal at the vanilla MoveSpeed stat baseline
  (verified via Pawn.TicksPerMove, Verse/Pawn.cs:3221 — 60/MoveSpeed with
  MoveSpeed≈4.6c/s ≈ 13). pathCost 3 reads 13/16 ≈ 81% for a standard-speed
  pawn — matching "~80%" — and because Pawn_PathFollower.CostToMoveIntoCell
  (Verse/AI/Pawn_PathFollower.cs:746) ADDS pathCost to the pawn's own base
  cost rather than multiplying it, actualCost is STRICTLY greater than the
  pawn's own unencumbered cost for every pawn regardless of stats — "never
  full speed" is a structural guarantee, not a tuned average, exactly the
  spec's own wording ("never full speed ever").

  Flammability 0 (omitted, defaults to 0) — cooled/spent bitumen glass does
  not reignite, matching SUMP_TAR_HYDROLOGY_1 ruling 3 ("flood fronts cool
  to glass") and RUT_TarSpent's own already-shipped "burnt tar is spent"
  precedent; since it is never flammable no burnedDef is required.

  costList: Chemfuel, 6/cell — PLACEHOLDER, same posture and same stand-in
  RUT_TarMoat.xml's own header already used for "barreled tar" (checked
  again this pass: still does not exist). ⚠️ OWED: re-point at the real
  bitumen/dig-barrel chain (korveth nodules, dig barrels — item spec §3)
  once the items pass ships it.

  Texture reuses RM_TarDeep's own texturePath (WaterDeepRamp) — same
  "poured/cooled tar IS the same substance" reasoning RUT_TarMoat.xml
  already gives for reusing that path — tinted with a slight sheen instead
  of RUT_TarMoat's flat matte black, to read as glazed rather than wet.

  Both tiers: designationCategory Floors, terrainAffordanceNeeded Light,
  affordances Light+Medium+Walkable (matches this kit's own established
  "platform, not bunker" convention — RUT_Boughway/RUT_BoughSoil — INVENTED
  judgment call for THIS kit, not inherited from a hard ban). Ship-buildable
  aboard the gravship (BIOME_SHIP_CONTRIBUTIONS_1) needs no extra wiring:
  an ordinary Light-affordance floor terrain is buildable anywhere a floor
  already is, gravship deck included.

  🔑 The slip-and-fall comp (glasswalk only) is generic, not glasswalk-
  specific: RM_MapComponent_GlasswalkSlip (RimMandrake.EnvironmentalHazards,
  this same build pass) watches for the tag below on ANY terrain, so a
  future biome's own "slick floor" can opt in with just this tag and no new
  C#.
  ============================================================================
-->
<Defs>

  <TerrainDef>
    <defName>RUT_Duckboards</defName>
    <label>duckboards</label>
    <description>Cheap brindeth-plank walkway laid straight over the muck. Fast when fresh — but the black tracks in on every boot, and a fouled board is a slow board until someone scrubs it. Dry planking over a tar biome; treat fire seriously.</description>
    <renderPrecedence>250</renderPrecedence>
    <texturePath>Terrain/Surfaces/WoodFloor</texturePath>
    <edgeType>FadeRough</edgeType>
    <fertility>0</fertility>
    <layerable>true</layerable>
    <color>(96,88,72)</color>
    <pathCost>0</pathCost>
    <designationCategory>Floors</designationCategory>
    <terrainAffordanceNeeded>Light</terrainAffordanceNeeded>
    <affordances>
      <li>Light</li>
      <li>Medium</li>
      <li>Walkable</li>
    </affordances>
    <burnedDef>BurnedWoodPlankFloor</burnedDef>
    <costList>
      <WoodLog>4</WoodLog>
    </costList>
    <statBases>
      <WorkToBuild>110</WorkToBuild>
      <Flammability>1.0</Flammability>
      <Beauty>-2</Beauty>
    </statBases>
  </TerrainDef>

  <TerrainDef>
    <defName>RUT_Glasswalk</defName>
    <label>glasswalk</label>
    <description>Bitumen poured, packed, and let cool to a hard black sheen. Nothing sticks to it, nothing tracks off it, and it never needs a scrub — but glass underfoot never lets you run flat out, and a hurrying pawn occasionally finds that out the hard way.</description>
    <renderPrecedence>251</renderPrecedence>
    <texturePath>Terrain/Surfaces/WaterDeepRamp</texturePath>
    <edgeType>FadeRough</edgeType>
    <fertility>0</fertility>
    <layerable>true</layerable>
    <color>(20,19,22)</color>
    <pathCost>3</pathCost>
    <designationCategory>Floors</designationCategory>
    <terrainAffordanceNeeded>Light</terrainAffordanceNeeded>
    <affordances>
      <li>Light</li>
      <li>Medium</li>
      <li>Walkable</li>
    </affordances>
    <filthAcceptanceMask>
      <li>None</li>
    </filthAcceptanceMask>
    <tags>
      <li>RM_SlipperyWalkway</li>
    </tags>
    <costList>
      <Chemfuel>6</Chemfuel>
    </costList>
    <statBases>
      <WorkToBuild>240</WorkToBuild>
      <Flammability>0</Flammability>
      <Beauty>1</Beauty>
    </statBases>
  </TerrainDef>

</Defs>
